From 7fba93e692040eeb2ba9150646a77fc4398f057f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 03:18:54 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] Show the "Stereo 3D" canvas as a surface of its own on
 Wayland

Under Qt's Wayland platform the surface of the main window is never
stereo: a stereo window has its whole interface doubled with the
picture. The canvas of a side-by-side pair is shown to the two eyes
through KisStereoCanvasLayer instead, a subsurface placed below the
window's surface at the rectangle of the canvas widget, with left and
right buffers (the compositor shows it to each eye, in place, and the
window stays one picture at one eye's size). It has the canvas's
surface format, so a stereo canvas keeps the depth of the canvas.

The renderer paints each eye into a texture of its own
(paintCanvasToEyeTexture), the canvas widget hands the two textures to
the layer, which copies them into its left and right buffer, and draws
nothing but the decorations and the brush outline itself, over
transparency: they are on the screen plane, the same in both eyes.
The main window has an alpha buffer for the layer to show through
(8 bits per channel: the 16-bit surfaces have no alpha), paints its own
background except under the canvas, and takes the format only for
itself: the format the canvases take is unchanged. A canvas does not
take a surface of its own for colour management while the layer is in
use, since that one has no alpha either.

The stereo canvas no longer keeps Krita on xcb under a Wayland
session: the native Wayland platform is chosen for it whatever the
depth. On Qt's xcb platform it stays as it was, with left and right
buffers on the default surface format.

Assisted-by: Claude Sonnet via Claude Code
